  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Wed Jul 1 2026\n\nMid to upper level high pressure remains strong in the front half of \nthe extended period. As we head into next week, the upper level \npattern takes on more of a quasi-zonal flow regime, with weak \ntroughing expected through much of the CONUS. Ambient temperatures \nare expected to rise both Friday and Saturday. Upper 90s and \npossibly some lower 100s are expected for highs across the CWA, \nexcept for the mountains. Extreme heat is still in question due \nlower forecast dewpoints, but with Tds ranging in the 60s, heat \nindex values will still be the primary concern. Maximum heat indices \nwill likely still range in the upper 90s and lower 100s Friday \nthrough Sunday, with isolated areas breaching over 105 degrees. High \ntemperature trends may decrease heading into next week, with a north-\nto-south progression showing from the latest NBM guidance. Heat \nindices may still be an issue though, as forecast dewpoint \ntemperatures look to increase once again.\n\nPoPs continue to trend upward as well, with chance PoPs (>25%) more \nwidespread across the CWA Sunday onward. The overall convective \npattern is not expected to change much, so pulse-type convection \nwith isolated to scattered coverage is anticipated for the coming \nafternoons and evenings. Of course, a low end severe threat may also \nlinger for the coming days with the potential for damaging winds as \nthe primary risk factor.\n\nRangel&&",
